[Thinking]
**Considering input design**

I need an HTML/CSS/JS example where an icon is placed inside an input wrapper, positioned absolutely on the right. It sounds like I could use a button or an SVG in a span for this. Also, I’ll need to make sure there's an onclick handler attached to it. This will allow the user to interact with the icon easily. Let’s see how best to implement that!
[/Thinking]

You can wrap the input and the SVG in a container, then absolutely-position the icon on the right and attach an `onclick` handler.

Here's a minimal example:

```html
<style>
  .input-wrapper {
    position: relative;
    display: inline-block; /* or block, as you prefer */
  }

  .input-with-icon {
    padding-right: 2.2rem; /* make room for the icon on the right */
    box-sizing: border-box;
  }

  .input-icon {
    position: absolute;
    right: 0.5rem;
    top: 50%;
    transform: translateY(-50%);
    width: 1.2rem;
    height: 1.2rem;
    cursor: pointer;
    fill: #555; /* for inline svg */
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
  }

  .input-icon:hover {
    fill: #000;
  }
</style>

<div class="input-wrapper">
  <input
    id="search-input"
    class="input-with-icon"
    type="text"
    placeholder="Search..."
  />

  <!-- clickable icon -->
  <div class="input-icon" onclick="onSearchClick()">
    <!-- example magnifying glass SVG -->
    <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24">
      <path
        d="M15.5 14h-.79l-.28-.27a6.471 6.471 0 0 0 1.57-4.23
           6.5 6.5 0 1 0-6.5 6.5 6.471 6.471 0 0 0 4.23-1.57l.27.28v.79
           L20 20.49 21.49 19 15.5 14zm-6 0C7.01 14 5 11.99 5 9.5
           S7.01 5 9.5 5 14 7.01 14 9.5 11.99 14 9.5 14z"
      />
    </svg>
  </div>
</div>

<script>
  function onSearchClick() {
    const value = document.getElementById('search-input').value;
    // Your logic here
    console.log('Search clicked with value:', value);
  }
</script>
```

Key points:
- The wrapper (`.input-wrapper`) is `position: relative`.
- The input has extra `padding-right` so text doesn’t overlap the icon.
- The SVG container (`.input-icon`) is absolutely positioned on the right and made clickable with `onclick`.
- In `onSearchClick()`, you can read the input value and perform your search or other logic.

If you share your current HTML/CSS, I can adapt this exactly to your setup.

